WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.301 --> 00:00:08.466
Captain Nemo and I returned to the gallery, where we settled ourselves on one of the couches. He showed me a set of drawings of the Nautilus

2
00:00:08.468 --> 00:00:19.697
. "As you can see, Professor Aronnax," said the captain, "my ship is shaped like a cigar, measuring 232 feet in length and 26 feet at its widest

3
00:00:19.698 --> 00:00:26.618
point. It has two steel hulls joined together, which make it as strong as if it were made of solid

4
00:00:26.619 --> 00:00:30.935
metal." "How does it sink under the water?" I asked, studying the

5
00:00:30.936 --> 00:00:36.927
plans. "The submarine has reservoirs that can be filled with seawater when we need to descend," he

6
00:00:36.928 --> 00:00:42.911
explained. "But we don't have to take on a huge amount of water because everything has a natural tendency to sink in

7
00:00:42.912 --> 00:00:46.848
water." "Then how do you make the submarine rise to the

8
00:00:46.849 --> 00:00:50.855
surface?" "All we have to do is pump out the extra water," said the captain.

9
00:00:50.856 --> 00:00:54.601
"The pumps, which run on electricity, are very powerful.

10
00:00:54.602 --> 00:00:59.427
But I'm sure you observed that when jets of water burst over the Abraham Lincoln

11
00:00:59.428 --> 00:01:01.673
." I remembered that quite

12
00:01:01.674 --> 00:01:08.078
well. "The submarine also has rudders on either side that help to guide it up or down," the captain went

13
00:01:08.079 --> 00:01:11.713
on. "But how can the pilot see where he's going?" I

14
00:01:11.714 --> 00:01:18.805
asked. "He sits in a glass pilot house on top of the vessel. Behind him is a powerful electric light that shows him the

15
00:01:18.806 --> 00:01:28.983
way." "Aha!" I said. "That explains the glow of the 'narwhal' that puzzled us so. Well, Captain, your Nautilus is certainly a marvelous

16
00:01:28.984 --> 00:01:33.709
vessel." "It is indeed, Professor, and I love it as if it were a part of

17
00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:38.792
myself. I am the engineer, builder, and captain of this wonderful

18
00:01:38.793 --> 00:01:42.792
boat." "But how did you manage to build it in secret?" I

19
00:01:42.793 --> 00:01:46.634
asked. "Each part came from a different place," said Captain

20
00:01:46.635 --> 00:01:54.038
Nemo. "The keel was made in France, the propeller in Scotland, the engine in Prussia, and the instruments in New

21
00:01:54.039 --> 00:01:59.871
York. I gave a different name to every supplier, so that no one could trace the orders back to

22
00:01:59.872 --> 00:02:06.030
me." "Yes, but someone had to put the various parts together," I said, still

23
00:02:06.031 --> 00:02:14.704
mystified. "I set up my workshop on a desert island. There my crew, whom I had trained, put the Nautilus together under my

24
00:02:14.705 --> 00:02:18.030
direction. When we finished, we burned all traces of our

25
00:02:18.031 --> 00:02:24.752
work." I couldn't begin to imagine how much this vessel was worth, especially considering the value of the treasures

26
00:02:24.753 --> 00:02:29.601
onboard. "One last question, Captain Nemo," I said. "Are you

27
00:02:29.602 --> 00:02:35.776
rich?" "Immensely rich, Professor. I could pay the national debt of France and never miss the

28
00:02:35.777 --> 00:02:41.006
money." I stared at him. Was he pulling my leg? Time would tell.

29
00:02:41.007 --> 00:02:44.265
"Now," said Captain Nemo, "it's almost noon.

30
00:02:44.266 --> 00:02:51.236
Time for us to figure out our location and determine our starting point for this voyage. We need to go back up to the surface."

31
00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:54.165
The captain pressed a button three times.

32
00:02:54.166 --> 00:03:01.323
The pumps began to drive the water from the tanks; the needle on one of his instruments showed that we were rising to the surface.

33
00:03:01.324 --> 00:03:03.324
The needle stopped moving.

34
00:03:03.325 --> 00:03:09.402
"We've arrived," said the captain, who then led the way to a platform on top of the submarine.

35
00:03:09.403 --> 00:03:12.648
The platform was only three feet above the water.

36
00:03:12.649 --> 00:03:18.625
Toward the middle of the platform I saw the slight bump that indicated where the small boat was stored.

37
00:03:18.626 --> 00:03:28.974
I also observed the two glass enclosures that the captain had described, one for the pilot and the other for the electric light that guided him.

38
00:03:28.975 --> 00:03:37.038
The sea was beautiful and the sky was clear. A light breeze rippled the surface of the water. There was nothing in sight.

39
00:03:37.039 --> 00:03:43.363
Captain Nemo used his sextant to measure the altitude of the sun, thus determining our latitude.

40
00:03:43.364 --> 00:03:51.450
"It's noon," said the captain. "Professor, whenever you're ready . . ." He indicated the hatch that led to the submarine's interior.

41
00:03:51.451 --> 00:03:58.180
I took one last look at the sea and climbed down the ladder. I felt the vessel beginning to descend.

42
00:03:58.181 --> 00:04:06.196
In the gallery Captain Nemo used another instrument to calculate our longitude and determine our exact position on the sea.

43
00:04:06.197 --> 00:04:12.021
"Our voyage under the seas began at noon today, November 8," he said.

44
00:04:12.022 --> 00:04:20.513
"We are headed northeast at a depth of 156 feet. Here are some maps upon which you can follow our course." He bowed to me.

45
00:04:20.514 --> 00:04:23.577
"And now I'll leave you to your studies."

46
00:04:23.578 --> 00:04:28.744
I spent the next hour thinking about Captain Nemo and his amazing submarine. Then

47
00:04:28.745 --> 00:04:34.665
I noticed a large map on the table, and I put my finger on the spot where our voyage had begun. I

48
00:04:34.666 --> 00:04:40.498
realized that we were following the Black River, a powerful ocean current like the Gulf Stream. I

49
00:04:40.499 --> 00:04:46.236
was still staring at the map when Ned and Conseil appeared at the door of the gallery. They

50
00:04:46.237 --> 00:04:51.142
were standing still, frozen at the sight of the wonders spread before them. "Where

51
00:04:51.143 --> 00:04:56.292
are we?" cried Ned. "In a museum in Quebec?" "No,

52
00:04:56.293 --> 00:05:01.070
it must be a museum in Paris," insisted Conseil. I

53
00:05:01.071 --> 00:05:12.554
motioned for them to enter. "My friends, you are in neither Canada nor France but onboard the Nautilus , 156 feet below sea level."

54
00:05:12.555 --> 00:05:20.864
"Professor Aronnax," said Ned, "can you tell me how many men are onboard with us? Ten, twenty, fifty, a hundred?"

55
00:05:20.865 --> 00:05:29.054
"I don't know," I replied. "I think you should give up any idea of seizing the Nautilus or escaping from it right now.

56
00:05:29.055 --> 00:05:37.689
This ship is a masterpiece of modern industry, and I'm glad I have the chance to see it. So be quiet for a while and let's see what happens."

57
00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:44.610
"See!" exclaimed Ned. "But we can see nothing in this underwater prison! We're sailing blindly!"

58
00:05:44.611 --> 00:05:49.022
Ned had barely said these words when all the lights went out.

59
00:05:49.023 --> 00:05:53.268
The three of us didn't dare to speak; we didn't know what would happen next.

60
00:05:53.269 --> 00:05:56.768
We heard something that sounded like panels sliding.

61
00:05:56.769 --> 00:05:59.268
"It's the end!" cried Ned.

62
00:05:59.269 --> 00:06:05.173
Suddenly light appeared at either end of the gallery, thanks to two large windows.

63
00:06:05.174 --> 00:06:11.982
The sea was clearly visible for a mile around the Nautilus . What a

64
00:06:11.983 --> 00:06:17.563
spectacle! "Well, you wished to see, Ned," I said. "Now you can

65
00:06:17.564 --> 00:06:23.991
see." "Amazing! Truly amazing!" muttered the harpooner, forgetting his bad mood.

66
00:06:23.992 --> 00:06:27.903
Conseil was equally captivated by the view through the windows.

67
00:06:27.904 --> 00:06:33.308
As Ned named the fish, Conseil put them into their scientific classifications.

68
00:06:33.309 --> 00:06:43.317
I won't mention all the varieties that passed before my dazzled eyes for two hours, but they represented a superb collection of specimens that I had only read about.

69
00:06:43.318 --> 00:06:51.714
These fish, more numerous than the birds of the air, were undoubtedly attracted by the brilliance of the submarine's electric light.

70
00:06:51.715 --> 00:06:58.136
Suddenly there was light in the gallery, the panels closed, and the enchanting vision disappeared.

71
00:06:58.137 --> 00:07:02.224
I expected Captain Nemo to join us, but he didn't appear.

72
00:07:02.225 --> 00:07:08.953
Ned and Conseil returned to their cabin, and I returned to mine, where I found that my dinner was ready.

73
00:07:08.954 --> 00:07:13.192
I passed the evening reading, writing, and thinking.

74
00:07:13.193 --> 00:07:21.335
That night I slept deeply while the Nautilus glided rapidly through the current of the Black River.

